<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salic law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Pactus Legis Salicae, Lex Salica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salic law, or Pactus Legis Salicae, is the law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a>, probably first issued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> in the early 6th century. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, it consists mainly of fines, including the wergild owed for killing free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and the half-free. A clause excluding women from inheriting land was cited from the 14th century to bar women from the French throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salic-law.jpg" alt="Salic law" width="320" height="536" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jugement_de_Childebert_III_accordant_%C3%A0_l%27abbaye_de_Saint-Denis_la_terre_de_Hodenc-l%27Ev%C3%AAque_dans_l%27Oise.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
